      "content": "Absolutely. The 20th-century labor movement in the U.S., driven by unions, played a crucial role in building and protecting the middle class. By negotiating for better wages, benefits, and working conditions, unions helped to lift working-class families out of poverty. This, in turn, contributed to the growth of the American middle class. Additionally, unions provided workers with a collective voice, increasing their political power and influence. This resulted in greater democratic participation as working-class people gained a stronger ability to advocate for their interests. As a result, labor unions helped create a fairer economic system, ensuring more equity and protection for American workers.",
